# Frontend UI (Nuxt + Nuxt UI + Tailwind)

Tokens: `app/assets/css/main.css`, `app/assets/css/tailwind.css`, `app/assets/css/ui-overrides.css`.

## Component vs layout split

| Layer | Tool | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Interactive UI | **Nuxt UI** | `UButton`, `UInput`, `UAlert`, `UCard`, `UModal`, `USlideover` |
| Contextual labels & tags | **Nuxt UI** | `UChip` / `UBadge` for eyebrows, status pills, tag lists |
| Page structure | **Tailwind** | flex/grid, `min-h-dvh`, gaps, padding, responsive breakpoints |
| Typography | **Tailwind** | `font-sans` (Inter) for UI/body, `font-display` (Manrope) for headings |

## Rules

1. Reach for **Nuxt UI primitives first** for interactive controls — avoid raw `<button>` / `<input>` when a Nuxt UI equivalent exists.
2. Use **`UChip`** for contextual labels, eyebrows, status pills, and tag-like UI. Prefer `variant="subtle"` with token classes (`bg-muted`, `text-foreground`).
3. Use **Tailwind utility classes** on wrappers and via `class` / `ui` props for layout and token alignment.
4. Primary CTAs: **`AppActionButton`** — see [action-buttons](action-buttons.mdc).
5. Avoid scoped `<style>` blocks in Vue SFCs. Repeated app-wide overrides go in **`ui-overrides.css`** with `@layer components`.
6. Match sibling patterns before inventing new styling.

## Examples

```vue
<!-- Good: static eyebrow — UChip + tokens -->
<UChip variant="subtle" color="neutral" class="bg-muted font-medium text-foreground">
  my-nuxt-stack
</UChip>

<!-- Good: Tailwind structure + Nuxt UI card -->
<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-8">
  <UCard variant="outline" class="rounded-2xl">...</UCard>
</div>

<!-- Avoid: raw input when UInput fits -->
<input class="rounded-lg border px-3 py-2" />
```
